[size=14pt][b][ANN] BlockYard 0.1.3 — a WebGL renderer, the Sun as a sky, a galaxy forming behind the chart, and a companion node[/b][/size]

[b]Repository:[/b] [url]https://github.com/BobClawblaw/blockyard[/url]
[b]Release:[/b] [url]https://github.com/BobClawblaw/blockyard/releases/tag/v0.1.3[/url]
[b]npm:[/b] [tt]npm install -g blockyard[/tt]
[b]Licence:[/b] Apache-2.0

[img width=800]https://talkimg.com/images/2026/09/22/U02gx8.jpg[/img]

Five days after 0.1.2, here is 0.1.3, and it is two releases in one. The first half is the [b]skies and the renderer[/b]: every 3D board, sky and effect now draws on either of two renderers — Software, as before, or [b]WebGL[/b], the same picture on the graphics card, many times faster on a big display, with a glow of its own — and two new skies stand behind the boards. The [b]Formation[/b] is a galaxy assembling itself after the TNG50 film. The [b]Sun[/b] is our star as the Solar Dynamics Observatory sees it at 171 Å, built as a model rather than a picture: it turns faster at its equator than at its poles, its active regions carry sunspots and true three-dimensional coronal loops, it flares, its filaments erupt after the observatory's own films, and there is a slider from solar minimum to maximum. The block finishes were rebuilt, ball lightning throws real lightning, the supernova follows NASA's animation frame by frame, and the Markets page gained 1 h, 3 h and 12 h charts and a sixth exchange, Gemini. The second half is the release prepared on 2026-09-19 and held for this: the node BlockYard was built beside, [b]Bitcoin Machine Code[/b] — an experimental, Core-compatible node in x86-64 assembly — named as its first-class companion, the RPC lane four calls wide, and the chart fixes that came from watching a node in initial sync beside a synced one.

If you are new here: BlockYard is a self-hosted web app you point at [b]your own Bitcoin node[/b]. You get a live dashboard of the node and the network, a 3D view of the block space being built in the mempool, a block, transaction and address explorer with its own address index, a mining dashboard, opt-in exchange prices, and a kiosk view. It is plain JavaScript on Node.js with [b]zero dependencies[/b] and [b]zero telemetry[/b], read-only toward your node, and it ships hardened: bound to this machine, sign-in on, HTTPS with a certificate it makes itself.

[size=12pt][b]The Sun, the Formation, and WebGL[/b][/size]

[img width=800]https://talkimg.com/images/2026/09/22/U02Krw.jpg[/img]

[b]The Sun[/b] is a fragment shader on its own canvas under the board, and everything in it is a model with a measurement behind it (the research is [tt]docs/PLAN-SUN-SKY.md[/tt]). The surface turns by the measured rotation law — the equator in one period, latitude sixty in a longer one — about an axis tipped 7.25°, darkened toward the limb by the same law the observatory measures. Ten active regions are carried round at their own latitude's rate: each has sunspots with a striated penumbra, plage, and a fan of coronal loops that are real arches standing off the sphere — they foreshorten as a region turns, cross in front of one another, are hidden by the sun, and stand tall against the dark on the limb. They flare, with a flash at the loop tops, the telescope's own diffraction cross on the big ones, and a wave that spreads over the disk. Their filaments erupt after the films of the 21 March 2024 double eruption: a dark thread lights and lifts, then is flung off as a red arch behind a bright front, leaving a cavity, ribbons and an arcade of loops — and about half fail and fall back. Quiet prominences stand red on the limb and show as dark filaments in front of the disk; rain condenses in the loops and streaks down their legs; the corona has helmet streamers, polar plumes and wind. A slider runs the eleven-year cycle from a nearly blank disk with great polar holes to a storm, another makes the sun fill the whole panel as a background, and five other SDO channels (304 red, 193 bronze, 211 violet, 131 teal, white light) are palettes. It ships dim, so a chart in front of it always wins.

[img width=800]https://talkimg.com/images/2026/09/22/U028P3.jpg[/img]

[b]The Formation[/b] is the TNG50 film's own view — gas streaming in along filaments as a glowing density field, settling into a thin turning disc, satellite galaxies falling in on new orbits, shedding wakes and stirring the gas they cross. It never loops and never fades. Thirteen palettes, twelve of them measured to stay clear of the chart's colours.

[img width=800]https://talkimg.com/images/2026/09/22/U02bO9.jpg[/img]

[b]WebGL.[/b] The viewer's drawing calls were implemented a second time on WebGL2, so every one of the thirty-odd effects runs on both renderers without a rewrite, and a browser without WebGL2 stays on Software by itself. On an RTX 5090 at 2560×1300 a resting Block space board went from 32 ms a frame to 1; the supernova from 90 to 10. WebGL has a glow of its own — neon lines, stars, sparks and white-hot cores throw real light — and the blocks never glow, because their colour is the feerate. The Galaxy sky's nebulae became billowing gas with dust lanes; the finishes were rebuilt: [b]chrome[/b] is polished steel reflecting a room, [b]satin[/b] brushed metal with machined bevels, and [b]neon[/b] tubes are one soft stroke with a white core.

[img width=800]https://talkimg.com/images/2026/09/22/U024iG.jpg[/img]

[b]Effects.[/b] Ball lightning throws lightning that behaves like lightning — channels that hold their shape while they slam on, re-strike and die, forking into hair-thin branches — and on Markets it flies in from beyond the screen's edge. The supernova on WebGL follows NASA's animation frame by frame: a smooth white-hot breakout with a cyan rim opening into lavender and violet billows round a magenta heart. The pulsar wind crosses the whole height of the board.

[img width=800]https://talkimg.com/images/2026/09/22/U029Nf.jpg[/img]

[b]Markets[/b] has [b]1 h, 3 h and 12 h[/b] charts — sixty one-minute, thirty-six five-minute and forty-eight fifteen-minute bars, fetched only while a chart that short is open — and [b]Gemini[/b] as a sixth exchange. The Kiosk follows whatever the Markets page chose, on every screen, within seconds.

[img width=800]https://talkimg.com/images/2026/09/22/U02B4Z.jpg[/img]

[size=12pt][b]Built alongside Bitcoin Machine Code[/b][/size]

[url=https://github.com/BobClawblaw/bitcoinmachinecode]Bitcoin Machine Code[/url] ([tt]bmc[/tt]) is a full validating Bitcoin node for Linux x86-64, written in NASM assembly with a C orchestration layer. Like BlockYard, every line of it is AI-authored. It verifies the chain from genesis, keeps a UTXO set proven byte-identical to Bitcoin Core's (MuHash-exact), follows mainnet live, runs a Core-policy mempool and relay, and serves Bitcoin Core's JSON-RPC.

[b]It is an academic, experimental development build, not ready for primetime.[/b] It has had internal and external security review but no independent human audit of its consensus and cryptographic assembly. Run it to study and evaluate, on a machine you can afford to lose, with no funds near it. For a production node, run Bitcoin Core. It is getting more solid every day, and BlockYard is how that is watched and measured.

What BlockYard does for it:

• [b]Its log is read, not just its RPC — every line of it.[/b] On the three current logs on this box — a node syncing, a synced one, and production — BlockYard now reads or deliberately sets aside 100.00% of the lines bmc writes, up from 53% a release ago. There is a rule per line shape and no catch-alls, so a line the node adds later still shows up as unread. It parses more than twice as fast as before, too: each line tries only its own tag's rules. That feeds the download window, the index builders, UTXO merges, the orphan pool and 1-parent-1-child relay, which peer announced each block, and now the node's boot sequence — things no RPC call returns.
• [b]Its RPC is measured against Core's.[/b] Every one of the 155 methods in Core's [tt]help[/tt] answers on bmc. Of 18 calls compared field by field, 14 return exactly Core's fields and two more differ only by fields of Core's unreleased development build. What is left is a short, stated list: a few refusals by design (no OpenRPC description, no asmap, no assumeutxo, and what its separate download process owns) and a handful of [tt]getpeerinfo[/tt] fields.
• [b]Its own indexes are asked first.[/b] The explorer asks the node's address index before anything else; bmc has one, Core does not.
• [b]It runs mempool.space out of the box.[/b] mempool.space needs a Core-compatible RPC and an Esplora REST API. On Core that REST API is a second program with an index of its own. bmc serves both from the node process, and builds every index mempool uses during its initial sync — no Electrum server, no separate indexer, no rebuild afterwards, no patch to mempool. (Set the index options before the first sync.)
• [b]A bmc node joins the node picker once it is 100% synced.[/b] One still in initial sync is a benchmark run, and stays out of the list until it finishes.

[img width=800]https://talkimg.com/images/2026/09/22/U027uD.jpg[/img]

[size=12pt][b]The RPC lane, four calls wide[/b][/size]

[tt]rpc.maxInFlight[/tt] had been advisory since it was written: the lane ran one call at a time, because the first node BlockYard watched could serve only one connection. Neither node here works that way any more. Eight [tt]getblockstats[/tt] at once ran at 4.9 times the throughput of one at a time on bmc, and 4.5 times on Bitcoin Core 31.1, whose default is four RPC threads. So the lane now keeps up to four calls in flight per node.

It does not ask any node for more calls a second: the starts are still spaced by the same rate ceiling. What changes is that one slow call — a mempool read, a block drill-down — no longer holds every other panel behind it. A node that really does serve one connection at a time gets [tt]"rpc": { "maxInFlight": 1 }[/tt] on its own entry.

[size=12pt][b]Charts, and a node in initial sync[/b][/size]

Watching a node in initial sync beside a synced one showed how many charts only told the truth for a synced node:

• [b]The synced node's block charts were wiped by the syncing one.[/b] Every node shares one block history, stamped with each block's own time. A node in initial sync added blocks from a year ago after the synced node's current ones, and the history — which searched on the assumption that it was in time order — read nothing back and deleted everything at its next cleanup. Late rows are now put in their place, and rows older than the retention window are not stored at all.
• [b]The syncing node's block charts were empty, then two clusters with a line across days.[/b] They plotted the last 24 hours by block time, which a node applying blocks from years ago does not have. For a node in initial sync they now draw a sample of the whole chain against height: three hundred blocks from the first to the tip, fetched once at the lowest priority, plus every block the polls fetch. Every point is a block the node reported.
• [b]The Network page's Throughput card was empty, and its figure was wrong.[/b] It had moved from the Overview keeping its Overview ids, so nothing drew it; and a log figure that is zero on a synced node had been overwriting the rate [tt]getnettotals[/tt] measures. A test now fails whenever a page writes to an element the page does not have. It found two more.
• [b]Switching node showed the old figures.[/b] Every switch now pulls at once, and an answer for the node you just left is dropped.
• [b]The node's own progress rows were blank[/b], because they read field names the monitor never stored. [b]About showed the node's version as "–"[/b], because the snapshot carried two keys called [tt]network[/tt]. Both are fixed.

[img width=800]https://talkimg.com/images/2026/09/22/U02ovj.jpg[/img]

[size=12pt][b]Diversions[/b][/size]

• [b]Wolfenstein 3D's menu takes keys again.[/b] The emulated mouse driver answered "where is the pointer" with 0,0, which the game's menu read as "up, held" and waited on forever. The driver now keeps a real pointer — which also lets the mouse move the menu, as it did in 1992.
• [b]Arrows and W A S D both move, and A and D strafe.[/b] The game has one key per direction and no strafe keys, so the page points each direction at whichever key you are holding, and makes A or D the strafe button while it is down. Letters still type where the game asks for a name.
• [b]Every shareware game ships whole, as its official release did.[/b] Quake 1.06 and DOOM 1.9 were already byte-identical to id's own archives. Wolfenstein 3D was missing Apogee's [tt]VENDOR.DOC[/tt], the licence its redistribution depends on, and a file that licence requires; both are added from the release 3D Realms distributed. [tt]NOTICE[/tt] names each game, its owner and its terms, and a test pins every game file to the hash of its official release.
• [b]The galaxy reaches the far side of a wide panel.[/b]

[size=12pt][b]Upgrading from 0.1.2[/b][/size]

[tt]npm install -g blockyard[/tt] (or [tt]git pull[/tt]), then restart.

• [b]The RPC lane keeps up to four calls in flight per node.[/b] If you watch a node that serves one connection at a time, give its entry [tt]"rpc": { "maxInFlight": 1 }[/tt].
• [b]One more outbound host, only with market polling on:[/b] [tt]api.gemini.com[/tt], for the sixth exchange.
• [b]Nothing to migrate.[/b] No setting was renamed and no file moved. The renderer ships on Software; WebGL is a choice in Display settings, Appearance.

[size=12pt][b]How it was made[/b][/size]

As before, [b]every line was written by an AI, directed by a human[/b], including the fixes, the tests and this post. The operator decided what mattered, what was wrong and what looked bad, and never typed a line of it. It is still [b]experimental pre-release software[/b]. Expect bugs, and tell us what broke.

